-- inventories-net-upstream-materials-and-spares
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Inventory accounting answers three questions in order: which goods belong to the entity, what they cost, and whether that cost will come back. Control settles the first, the cost elements and a cost formula the second, and the lower of cost and net realisable value (NRV) the third.</p></li><li><p>A cost formula only divides one pool of cost between the balance sheet and profit or loss. When prices rise, FIFO leaves the most on the balance sheet and weighted average less. LIFO, which leaves the least, is no longer allowed.</p></li><li><p>Not every industry follows the cost model. Agricultural produce enters inventory at fair value less costs to sell at harvest (PSAK 241), commodity broker-traders may carry stock at fair value less costs to sell, and producers of minerals and crops may carry it at NRV. PHE's materials follow the plain cost model, with an allowance of 45,911 against 852,308 of cost at the end of 2024.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report inventories, net, of 806,397 at 31 December 2024 against 658,712 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line is 2.6 percent of total assets and 10.0 percent of current assets (2.1 and 6.7 percent a year earlier).</p><p>After reading it a student should be able to explain each step of inventory accounting, record it in journals, and see where other industries depart from the cost model.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. What inventory is</h2><p>PSAK 202 <em>Inventories</em> (formerly PSAK 14, IAS 2) defines it in three parts: assets held for sale in the ordinary course of business, assets in the process of production for such sale, and materials or supplies to be consumed in production or in rendering services (para. 6).</p><p>The three parts follow three business models. A trader holds <em>merchandise</em>; a manufacturer holds <em>raw materials</em>, <em>work in process</em> and <em>finished goods</em>; a service or extractive business holds the <em>materials and supplies</em> it consumes.</p><p>PHE is the third kind. Its policy names "spare parts, chemicals and others" (Note 3j, Daftar 5/53, PDF p. 70), and Note 11 shows one category, general materials, held by the subsidiaries (Daftar 5/99–5/100, PDF pp. 116–117). There is no crude oil in the line. The revenue policy explains why little would be expected: PHE recognises crude revenue "based on the actual lifting amount and sold to customers" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79), so production cost goes to expense with the barrels sold instead of collecting as a stock of product.</p><p>Inventory is current even if part of it will be used after twelve months, because it is consumed in the normal operating cycle (PSAK 201.66(a) and 68, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1). Spare parts and stand-by equipment used over more than one period are fixed assets under PSAK 216 (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16, para. 8). A drill bit used up in one well is inventory; a spare compressor that will run for ten years is not.</p><h2>2. Perpetual and periodic systems</h2><p>In a <em>perpetual system</em> the inventory account moves with every receipt and issue, so the balance is known at any moment. In a <em>periodic system</em> purchases go to a <em>purchases</em> account and inventory is updated only after a year-end count, so usage is a residual.</p><blockquote><p>Usage = opening inventory + purchases − closing inventory (counted)</p></blockquote><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary opens a new shore base with no stock, buys 3,000 of drilling chemicals, and issues 2,400 to operations according to its issue slips. The year-end count finds 560.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Perpetual</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Periodic</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Purchase</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Inventories 3,000 / Cr Trade payables 3,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Purchases 3,000 / Cr Trade payables 3,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Issues</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Production expenses 2,400 / Cr Inventories 2,400</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year end</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Book 600, count 560: Dr Inventory shortage 40 / Cr Inventories 40</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Usage = 0 + 3,000 − 560 = 2,440: Dr Inventories 560, Dr Production expenses 2,440 / Cr Purchases 3,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both end with 560 on the balance sheet. Only the perpetual system shows that 40 went missing and puts it on a line someone has to explain; the periodic system buries it in usage. That is why Kieso stresses that a perpetual system still needs counts. The ledger says what should be there, and only the count says what is.</p><h2>3. Which goods are included</h2><p>The rule is control, not location. Kieso gives the standard cases.</p><ul><li><p><em>Goods in transit</em> follow the shipping terms. Under FOB (free on board) shipping point, title, freight and risk pass to the buyer when the seller hands the goods to the carrier, so goods at sea are the buyer's. Under FOB destination they stay the seller's until they arrive.</p></li><li><p><em>Consigned goods</em> stay with the consignor. The consignee holds them but does not control them.</p></li><li><p><em>Special agreements</em> need a closer look. A sale with a buy-back obligation may leave the goods with the seller, and under a sale with a right of return the seller removes the goods but records a refund liability and an asset for the right to recover them (PSAK 115).</p></li></ul><p>So a PHE subsidiary counts casing on a ship bought FOB shipping point although nobody has seen it, and leaves out a supplier's consigned chemicals although they sit in its warehouse.</p><p>PHE's contracts show the principle working against legal title. Under its production sharing contracts (PSCs), "materials, supplies and equipment acquired by the PSC Contractors … belongs to the Government", while the contractor has "the right to utilise" them until SKK Migas, the upstream regulator, approves them as surplus or abandoned (Note 2a(vii), Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). PHE still carries them as inventory, consistent with control: the contractor decides where each joint of pipe goes and consumes it.</p><h2>4. Which costs are included</h2><p>Cost is everything it took to bring an item to its present location and condition (PSAK 202.10). Kieso splits costs into two families. <em>Product costs</em> attach to the goods: purchase price, import duties and taxes that are not recoverable (para. 11), transport and other directly attributable costs, less trade discounts (para. 11), plus conversion costs for a manufacturer (section 8). <em>Period costs</em> are expensed as incurred: selling, administrative and general costs, and storage after arrival unless production needs it (para. 16).</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys 600 joints of casing from an overseas mill, FOB shipping point, list price USD 2.50 thousand per joint, with a 4 percent trade discount.</p><blockquote><p>Invoice = 600 × 2.50 × (1 − 4%) = 1,440</p><p>Cost at 31 December, at sea = 1,440 + 72 ocean freight and insurance = 1,512</p><p>Cost on arrival = 1,512 + 18 non-recoverable import charges + 30 port handling and trucking = 1,560</p></blockquote><p>Cash discounts for prompt payment follow one of two methods. The <em>gross method</em> records the invoice amount and deducts the discount only when earned. The <em>net method</em> records the discounted price from the start, on the view that it is the real price, so a missed discount is a finance cost under other income and expense. Suppose terms of 2/10, net 30, so paying within ten days saves 1,440 × 2% = 28.8.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gross method</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net method</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Purchase</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Inventories 1,440 / Cr Trade payables 1,440</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Inventories 1,411.2 / Cr Trade payables 1,411.2</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid within 10 days</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 1,440 / Cr Inventories 28.8 / Cr Cash 1,411.2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 1,411.2 / Cr Cash 1,411.2</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid late</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 1,440 / Cr Cash 1,440</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Trade payables 1,411.2, Dr Purchase discounts lost 28.8 / Cr Cash 1,440</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Paid on time, both reach a shelf cost of 1,560 − 28.8 = 1,531.2, or USD 2.552 thousand per joint. Paid late, the gross method leaves 28.8 inside the casing, while the net method shows it as the cost of financing the purchase. Kieso prefers the net method. PSAK 202 does not settle the choice: its interest rule (para. 18) covers settlement beyond normal credit terms, and day 30 of net 30 is within them. IFRS practice deducts discounts taken and generally leaves a missed discount in cost, the gross-method result.</p><h2>5. Cost formulas</h2><p>When identical items are bought at different prices, which price leaves with an issue? A cost formula answers that. It does not change the total; it only splits one pool of cost between what is used up and what stays on the shelf. PSAK 202 allows three answers (paras. 23–25).</p><ul><li><p><em>Specific identification</em> tracks each item's actual cost. It is required for items that are not interchangeable or are set aside for one project, such as a wellhead built for one well.</p></li><li><p><em>First-in, first-out (FIFO)</em> assumes the oldest items go first, so closing stock carries the latest prices.</p></li><li><p><em>Weighted average</em> gives every unit the pool's average cost, once for the period (periodic) or after every purchase (moving average, in a perpetual system).</p></li></ul><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary's tubing ledger for one year, in USD thousand per joint:</p><blockquote><p>1 January: on hand 200 joints at 1.20 = 240.00</p><p>10 March: buy 300 joints at 1.30 = 390.00</p><p>15 May: issue 350 joints to a routine workover (a repair of an existing well)</p><p>5 September: buy 250 joints at 1.45 = 362.50</p><p>20 November: issue 150 joints to a new development well</p><p>Cost available = 992.50 for 750 joints; closing stock 250 joints</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>FIFO: issues = 200 × 1.20 + 150 × 1.30 + 150 × 1.30 = 630.00; closing = 250 × 1.45 = 362.50</p><p>Periodic average: 992.50 / 750 = 1.32333; issues = 500 × 1.32333 = 661.67; closing = 250 × 1.32333 = 330.83</p><p>Moving average: after March (240.00 + 390.00) / 500 = 1.26, so May issue = 441.00 and 189.00 remains; after September (189.00 + 362.50) / 400 = 1.37875, so November issue = 206.81 and closing = 344.69</p></blockquote><p>All three split the same 992.50. With prices rising, FIFO leaves the most on the shelf and the least in expense; the averages sit in between. LIFO would leave the oldest prices on the shelf (200 × 1.20 + 50 × 1.30 = 305.00, periodic), which is why IAS 2 removed it in 2003, because in a long inflation the balance sheet would carry prices from decades ago. Indonesian income tax law is narrower still and accepts only average and FIFO (UU PPh art. 10(6)).</p><p>The same formula must be used for inventories of similar nature and use across a group (paras. 25–26). An issue lands according to what the material does: the routine workover keeps a well producing, so its cost is a production expense, while the development well is a new asset, so its cost is capitalised into oil and gas properties and reaches profit later through depletion on a unit-of-production basis (para. 35). PHE uses the weighted average (Note 3j).</p><h2>6. Lower of cost and net realisable value</h2><p>Cost says what was spent to get an item onto the shelf, but not whether that spending will come back. The lower of cost and NRV rule closes that gap. An asset should not be carried at more than the entity expects to recover from it (para. 9), and a loss that has already happened belongs in this period.</p><blockquote><p>NRV = estimated selling price in the ordinary course of business − estimated costs to complete − estimated costs to sell</p></blockquote><p>There are only two possibilities. If NRV is above cost, nothing happens. The rule only writes down, and an expected gain waits for the sale. If NRV is below cost, the item is written down to NRV and the difference is expensed.</p><p><strong>Unit of comparison.</strong> The comparison is made item by item. Similar items may be grouped only when they cannot practicably be judged one by one, and writing down a whole class such as "all spares" is not allowed (para. 29). For example (illustration), suppose 51,000 of PHE's 852,308 of materials at cost (Note 11) is surplus that SKK Migas has approved for sale, so each item has a selling price.</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Item</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cost</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>NRV</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Lower of the two</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Drill pipe, 5-inch, grade G</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>11,200</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>11,200</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Drill pipe, 5-inch, grade S</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>30,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>30,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Gate valves, 3-inch</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>9,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>8,100</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>8,100</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>51,000</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>51,100</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>49,300</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Item by item: write-down = 51,000 − 49,300 = 1,700. As a whole: NRV 51,100 &gt; cost 51,000, no write-down</p></blockquote><p>Comparing totals would let 1,800 of headroom on grade S pipe hide 1,700 of losses on the other two items.</p><p><strong>Materials held for use.</strong> Most of PHE's stock is never sold, so there is no selling price to use. PSAK 202 looks at what the material turns into. Materials held for use in production are not written down below cost while the product they go into is expected to sell at or above cost (para. 32), because the material's value is recovered through the product. For an upstream group the "product" is the oil and gas the field produces, or the well the material builds. That is a reading by analogy, since that output never sits in inventory. Only when the product no longer covers its cost is the material written down, and replacement cost may then be the best available measure of its NRV. For example (illustration), drilling chemicals bought for 1,000 whose replacement price has fallen to 850.</p><blockquote><p>Field still economic: no write-down, carried at 1,000</p><p>Field no longer covers its cost: write-down = 1,000 − 850 = 150</p></blockquote><p>At an upstream group the test therefore bites mainly on stock that will not be used at all: obsolete grades, parts for retired equipment, materials that have sat too long (para. 28). PHE describes its allowance in those terms, for "obsolete, unuseable and slow-moving materials" (Note 3j).</p><p><strong>Recording the write-down.</strong> Kieso gives two methods that differ only in where the expense sits. The <em>COGS method</em> folds it into cost of goods sold, for PHE production expenses; the <em>loss method</em> shows it on its own line. Either way most entities credit an allowance, a contra account, so the item ledger stays at cost and the balance sheet shows the net figure. That is where the line gets the "net" in its name. Take PHE's 2024 numbers as the starting point (Note 11): cost 852,308, allowance needed at year end 45,911, opening allowance 37,639. Assume, for illustration, that the whole movement is new write-downs with no scrapping or reversals.</p><blockquote><p>Net charge for 2024 = 45,911 − 37,639 = 8,272; inventories, net = 852,308 − 45,911 = 806,397</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 50px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>COGS method</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Loss method</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Production expenses 8,272</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Loss due to decline in inventory value 8,272</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cr Allowance for decline in value 8,272</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cr Allowance for decline in value 8,272</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Profit and the net 806,397 are the same under both. PHE does not say which line carries the charge.</p><p><strong>Recovery and scrapping.</strong> When the reason for a write-down goes away, it is reversed, but only up to the original write-down, so an item never returns above cost (para. 33), and the reversal reduces the period's inventory expense (para. 34). For example (illustration), in 2025, slow-moving pipe carrying an allowance of 4,000 is taken up for a new well campaign.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Allowance for decline in value 4,000 / Cr Recovery of inventory loss 4,000</p></blockquote><p>If the pipe were scrapped at the value already provided for, cost and allowance would leave together (Dr Allowance and Dr Cash for any scrap proceeds / Cr Inventories) and profit would not move.</p><h2>7. Other valuation bases and estimation methods</h2><p><strong>Purchase commitments.</strong> A loss can arise before the goods arrive. Kieso recognises it when the market price falls below a non-cancellable contract price. PSAK reaches it through PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37): a contract is <em>onerous</em> when the unavoidable cost of meeting it exceeds the benefit expected from it (para. 10). For example (illustration), a subsidiary has a non-cancellable order for casing at 10,000 for 2025 delivery, and by the end of 2024 the market price is 7,000. If the casing is still going into economic wells, section 6 says it would not be written down on delivery, so nothing is booked. If the drilling programme has been cancelled, a provision of 3,000 is recognised.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Loss on onerous purchase commitment 3,000 / Cr Provision 3,000</p><p>On delivery: Dr Inventories 7,000, Dr Provision 3,000 / Cr Cash 10,000</p></blockquote><p><strong>Basket purchases.</strong> Items bought for one price share it by their <em>relative standalone sales value</em>. For example (illustration), a lot of used rig spares bought for 900, with standalone values of 600 for pumps and 300 each for valves and fittings (1,200 in total).</p><blockquote><p>Pumps = 900 × 600 / 1,200 = 450; valves = fittings = 900 × 300 / 1,200 = 225</p><p>Dr Inventories, pumps 450, Dr Inventories, valves 225, Dr Inventories, fittings 225 / Cr Cash 900</p></blockquote><p><strong>The gross profit method.</strong> When stock cannot be counted, after a fire or for an interim report, it is estimated from the normal margin. For example (illustration), opening stock 800, purchases 3,600, sales 5,000, normal gross margin 25 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Cost of goods sold = 5,000 × 75% = 3,750; closing inventory = 800 + 3,600 − 3,750 = 650</p></blockquote><p>It rests on a past margin that may no longer hold, so Kieso treats it as an estimate or a check, not a basis for annual statements.</p><p><strong>The retail method.</strong> Retailers apply the same logic through a ratio of cost to retail price, accepted where many fast-moving items share similar margins (para. 22). <em>Markups</em> raise retail prices above the original and <em>markdowns</em> cut them below it. For example (illustration), a retail subsidiary has opening stock of 50 at cost and 100 at retail, purchases of 2,000 at cost and 3,500 at retail, net markups of 200, net markdowns of 50 and sales of 2,500.</p><blockquote><p>Goods available: cost 2,050; retail 3,600 + 200 markups = 3,800</p><p>Ratio A (markups only) = 2,050 / 3,800 = 53.95%</p><p>Retail after markdowns = 3,800 − 50 = 3,750; ratio B = 2,050 / 3,750 = 54.67%</p><p>Closing stock at retail = 3,750 − 2,500 = 1,250</p><p>At cost: A = 1,250 × 53.95% = 674.3; B = 1,250 × 54.67% = 683.3</p></blockquote><p>Ratio A leaves markdowns out of the denominator, so it gives the lower closing stock, the conventional version that approximates the lower of cost and market.</p><p><strong>Errors.</strong> Closing inventory is subtracted to get the expense, so overstating it by 1,000 makes this year's profit 1,000 too high and, as next year's opening stock, next year's 1,000 too low. The error reverses over two years, but both years are misstated. A material error found later is corrected retrospectively under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8).</p><h2>8. Inventory across industries</h2><p><strong>Manufacturing.</strong> Cost includes <em>conversion costs</em>: direct labour and a systematic share of production overhead (para. 12). Fixed overhead is spread on <em>normal capacity</em>, not actual output, so a quiet year does not make each unit look dearer (para. 13). In an abnormally busy year the rate is reduced instead, so units are not carried above cost, and variable overhead follows actual use. For example (illustration), fixed overhead 1,200, normal capacity 1,000 units, actual output 800.</p><blockquote><p>Rate = 1,200 / 1,000 = 1.2 per unit; into inventory = 800 × 1.2 = 960; expensed as idle capacity = 1,200 − 960 = 240</p></blockquote><p>Standard costs may be used if they approximate actual cost and are reviewed regularly (para. 21).</p><p><strong>Agriculture.</strong> Living animals and plants are <em>biological assets</em> under PSAK 241 <em>Agriculture</em> (formerly PSAK 69, IAS 41), not inventory. They are measured at fair value less costs to sell (not NRV), with each period's change in profit or loss, because growth itself is how value is created. At harvest the produce becomes inventory, and its fair value less costs to sell at that moment becomes its cost under PSAK 202 (para. 20). Plants that only bear produce, such as oil palms, rubber or tea, are <em>bearer plants</em> and are fixed assets under PSAK 216; the produce growing on them stays in PSAK 241 until harvest. For example (illustration), a plantation harvests 10,000 tonnes of fresh fruit bunches when their fair value is 0.19 per tonne (USD 190) and costs to sell are 0.01 per tonne. The produce on the trees was carried at 1,500 at the last reporting date.</p><blockquote><p>Fair value less costs to sell at harvest = 10,000 × (0.19 − 0.01) = 1,800</p><p>Remeasure to harvest: Dr Biological assets 300 / Cr Gain on change in fair value less costs to sell 300</p><p>Harvest: Dr Inventories, fresh fruit bunches 1,800 / Cr Biological assets 1,800</p></blockquote><p>The 300 gain at harvest (IAS 41.28) recognises growth as it happens. From there the bunches are ordinary inventory, so milling into crude palm oil adds conversion costs to the 1,800, and the lower of cost and NRV applies.</p><p><strong>Producers of minerals and crops, and commodity traders.</strong> PSAK 202 exempts two groups from its measurement rules (para. 3). Producers of agricultural and forest products, of produce after harvest, and of minerals and mineral products may measure at NRV where that is established industry practice, for example when sale is assured by a forward contract or a government guarantee, or an active market exists (para. 4). Commodity broker-traders, who buy to profit from short-term price moves, may measure at fair value less costs to sell (para. 5), in both cases with changes in profit or loss.</p><p><strong>Mining.</strong> Ore stockpiles and concentrate are inventory at the lower of cost and NRV. In an open-pit mine the cost of removing waste rock during production is split between inventory, for ore extracted now, and a <em>stripping activity asset</em>, for access to ore later (ISAK 120, formerly ISAK 29, IFRIC 20). In Indonesia this replaced PSAK 33, which was revoked from 2014.</p><p><strong>Real estate.</strong> Land and buildings a developer holds for sale are inventory (para. 8). The same building would be investment property under PSAK 240 (formerly PSAK 13, IAS 40) if held for rent or capital appreciation, and a fixed asset under PSAK 216 if used by the entity. Intention decides the classification.</p><p><strong>Financial instruments</strong> are never inventory under PSAK 202 (para. 2(b)), even for a securities dealer; they follow PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9).</p><h2>9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 11</h2><p>PSAK 202 asks for the policy, the carrying amount by classification, the amount expensed, write-downs and reversals, and stock pledged (para. 36). PHE gives gross cost, the allowance and its movement (Note 11, Daftar 5/99, PDF p. 116).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>General materials, at cost</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>852,308</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>696,351</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Less allowance for decline in value</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(45,911)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(37,639)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Inventories, net</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>806,397</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>658,712</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Allowance coverage = 37,639 / 696,351 = 5.41% (2023); 45,911 / 852,308 = 5.39% (2024)</p></blockquote><p>The allowance grew in step with cost, so the 22.4 percent rise in the net line came from more materials at a steady coverage ratio. PHE prints one combined movement a year (−3,084 in 2023, +8,272 in 2024), and one net figure can hide several events. For example (illustration), the 2023 deduction of 3,084 could be all reversals (profit up 3,084), all scrapping of fully provided stock (profit unchanged), or new write-downs of 2,000 with 4,000 scrapped and 1,084 reversed (profit down 916).</p><blockquote><p>Inventory turnover = inventory expensed / average inventory; days in inventory = 365 / turnover</p></blockquote><p>For example (illustration), if PHE's subsidiaries had used 1,600,000 of materials in 2024, turnover would be 1,600,000 / ((806,397 + 658,712) / 2) = 2.18 times, about 167 days. PHE does not disclose the total amount of inventories expensed. Note 39 gives material usage of 163,694 for its other operations only (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166), and the cash flow statement uses the direct method (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17), so the real ratio cannot be computed. PHE also keeps 6,809 of "dead stock material" outside the line, in other non-current assets (Note 14b, Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122).</p><h2>10. Upstream note: cost recovery</h2><p>Under a cost recovery PSC, material costs are recovered from production (Note 2a(iii), Daftar 5/10, PDF p. 27). A deferred tax liability on "non-capital inventory" of 17,193 (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172) suggests such materials are deducted for PSC purposes before issue. Gross split contracts have no cost recovery (Note 2b, Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34). The inventory accounting is the same under both.</p><h2>11. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net">oil-gas-properties-net</a>: where materials issued to development wells are capitalised.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net">fixed-assets-net</a>: the boundary for major spares and stand-by equipment.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/production-expenses">production-expenses</a>: issued consumables and, under the COGS method, write-downs.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-payables">trade-payables</a>: the other side of purchases.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current">other-non-current-assets</a>: holds the dead stock material.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-liabilities</a>: the temporary difference on non-capital inventory.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>The control argument for carrying government-owned materials is this page's reading. PHE does not explain why it recognises them. That non-capital materials are recovered before they are issued is read from Note 43e, not stated in the FS, and a deferred tax liability can have other causes.</p></li><li><p>How PSAK 202 treats a missed settlement discount is not stated in the standard. The reading follows the 2004 IFRIC discussion and firm guidance.</p></li><li><p>Applying the PSAK 202 materials rule (para. 32) to an upstream group, where the product never sits in inventory, is a reading by analogy, as is using it to decide that the casing commitment is not onerous.</p></li><li><p>That little crude would be held at year end is inferred from the revenue policy. The FS state no policy for crude inventory.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 2a(iii) and 2a(vii) (Daftar 5/10–5/11, PDF pp. 27–28); Note 2b (Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34); Note 3j (Daftar 5/53, PDF p. 70); Note 3p (Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79); Note 11 (Daftar 5/99–5/100, PDF pp. 116–117); Note 14b (Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122); Note 39 (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes this series follows, "Inter 1 – Chapter 8 – Valuation of Inventories: A Cost-Basis Approach" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): classification, perpetual and periodic systems, inventory control, goods included, product and period costs, gross and net methods. NOTES. The notes do not cover cost formulas or inventory errors; section 5 and the errors paragraph follow Kieso (ref. 8) and IAS 2.23–27.</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes, "Inter 1 – Chapter 9 – Inventories: Additional Valuation Issues" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): lower of cost and NRV, COGS and loss methods, allowance, recovery, agricultural inventory, purchase commitments, relative standalone sales value, gross profit and retail methods. NOTES. Of section 8, only agriculture has a note; the rest follows IAS 2.2–5, 2.8, 2.12–13 and the standards named.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 202 <em>Persediaan</em> (IAS 2 <em>Inventories</em>), paras. 2–6, 8–13, 16, 18, 20–29, 32–36. VERIFIED (web, IAS 2 text reproduced at prg.kz; Grant Thornton IAS 2 factsheet). Settlement discounts: IFRS Interpretations Committee, "IAS 2 Discounts and rebates" (not added to agenda, Nov 2004); Grant Thornton IFRS viewpoint, "Inventory discounts and rebates". VERIFIED (web). The 2003 removal of LIFO is from knowledge, NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 241 <em>Agrikultur</em> (IAS 41 <em>Agriculture</em>) and the 2014 bearer plants amendments to IAS 16 and IAS 41. Scope VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation, "Agriculture: Bearer Plants", June 2014); IAS 41.12–13 and 28 VERIFIED (web, IAS Plus; PKF; ACCA DipIFR). The FFB price is an illustration, not a market figure.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 216 <em>Aset Tetap</em> (IAS 16), para. 8; PSAK 201 <em>Penyajian Laporan Keuangan</em> (IAS 1), paras. 66(a), 68. VERIFIED (web, Ind AS 16 mirror; ifrscommunity.com).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 237 (IAS 37), paras. 10, 68–69: VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation 2020 amendment "Onerous Contracts"). ISAK 120 (IFRIC 20) and the revocation of PSAK 33 by PPSAK 12 from 2014: VERIFIED (web, IAI). PSAK 240 (IAS 40), PSAK 115 (IFRS 15), PSAK 109 (IFRS 9), PSAK 208 (IAS 8): numbers VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, chs. 8–9, the textbook the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>UU No. 7/1983 on Income Tax, as last amended by UU No. 7/2021 (HPP), art. 10(6). VERIFIED (web, secondary: DDTCNews, 14 Sep 2024).</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Inventory accounting answers three questions in order: which goods belong to the entity, what they cost, and whether that cost will come back. Control settles the first, the cost elements and a cost formula the second, and the lower of cost and net realisable value (NRV) the third.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A cost formula only divides one pool of cost between the balance sheet and profit or loss. When prices rise, FIFO leaves the most on the balance sheet and weighted average less. LIFO, which leaves the least, is no longer allowed.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Not every industry follows the cost model. Agricultural produce enters inventory at fair value less costs to sell at harvest (PSAK 241), commodity broker-traders may carry stock at fair value less costs to sell, and producers of minerals and crops may carry it at NRV. PHE's materials follow the plain cost model, with an allowance of 45,911 against 852,308 of cost at the end of 2024.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report inventories, net, of 806,397 at 31 December 2024 against 658,712 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line is 2.6 percent of total assets and 10.0 percent of current assets (2.1 and 6.7 percent a year earlier).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading it a student should be able to explain each step of inventory accounting, record it in journals, and see where other industries depart from the cost model.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What inventory is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 202 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Inventories","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 14, IAS 2) defines it in three parts: assets held for sale in the ordinary course of business, assets in the process of production for such sale, and materials or supplies to be consumed in production or in rendering services (para. 6).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The three parts follow three business models. A trader holds ","type":"text"},{"text":"merchandise","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"; a manufacturer holds ","type":"text"},{"text":"raw materials","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"work in process","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"finished goods","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"; a service or extractive business holds the ","type":"text"},{"text":"materials and supplies","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" it consumes.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE is the third kind. Its policy names \"spare parts, chemicals and others\" (Note 3j, Daftar 5/53, PDF p. 70), and Note 11 shows one category, general materials, held by the subsidiaries (Daftar 5/99–5/100, PDF pp. 116–117). There is no crude oil in the line. The revenue policy explains why little would be expected: PHE recognises crude revenue \"based on the actual lifting amount and sold to customers\" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79), so production cost goes to expense with the barrels sold instead of collecting as a stock of product.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Inventory is current even if part of it will be used after twelve months, because it is consumed in the normal operating cycle (PSAK 201.66(a) and 68, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1). Spare parts and stand-by equipment used over more than one period are fixed assets under PSAK 216 (formerly PSAK 16, IAS 16, para. 8). A drill bit used up in one well is inventory; a spare compressor that will run for ten years is not.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Perpetual and periodic systems","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In a ","type":"text"},{"text":"perpetual system","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" the inventory account moves with every receipt and issue, so the balance is known at any moment. In a ","type":"text"},{"text":"periodic system","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" purchases go to a ","type":"text"},{"text":"purchases","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" account and inventory is updated only after a year-end count, so usage is a residual.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Usage = opening inventory + purchases − closing inventory (counted)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary opens a new shore base with no stock, buys 3,000 of drilling chemicals, and issues 2,400 to operations according to its issue slips. The year-end count finds 560.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Perpetual","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Periodic","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Purchase","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventories 3,000 / Cr Trade payables 3,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Purchases 3,000 / Cr Trade payables 3,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Issues","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Production expenses 2,400 / Cr Inventories 2,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year end","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Book 600, count 560: Dr Inventory shortage 40 / Cr Inventories 40","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Usage = 0 + 3,000 − 560 = 2,440: Dr Inventories 560, Dr Production expenses 2,440 / Cr Purchases 3,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Both end with 560 on the balance sheet. Only the perpetual system shows that 40 went missing and puts it on a line someone has to explain; the periodic system buries it in usage. That is why Kieso stresses that a perpetual system still needs counts. The ledger says what should be there, and only the count says what is.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Which goods are included","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The rule is control, not location. Kieso gives the standard cases.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goods in transit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" follow the shipping terms. Under FOB (free on board) shipping point, title, freight and risk pass to the buyer when the seller hands the goods to the carrier, so goods at sea are the buyer's. Under FOB destination they stay the seller's until they arrive.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Consigned goods","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" stay with the consignor. The consignee holds them but does not control them.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Special agreements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" need a closer look. A sale with a buy-back obligation may leave the goods with the seller, and under a sale with a right of return the seller removes the goods but records a refund liability and an asset for the right to recover them (PSAK 115).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"So a PHE subsidiary counts casing on a ship bought FOB shipping point although nobody has seen it, and leaves out a supplier's consigned chemicals although they sit in its warehouse.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's contracts show the principle working against legal title. Under its production sharing contracts (PSCs), \"materials, supplies and equipment acquired by the PSC Contractors … belongs to the Government\", while the contractor has \"the right to utilise\" them until SKK Migas, the upstream regulator, approves them as surplus or abandoned (Note 2a(vii), Daftar 5/11, PDF p. 28). PHE still carries them as inventory, consistent with control: the contractor decides where each joint of pipe goes and consumes it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Which costs are included","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost is everything it took to bring an item to its present location and condition (PSAK 202.10). Kieso splits costs into two families. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Product costs","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" attach to the goods: purchase price, import duties and taxes that are not recoverable (para. 11), transport and other directly attributable costs, less trade discounts (para. 11), plus conversion costs for a manufacturer (section 8). ","type":"text"},{"text":"Period costs","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are expensed as incurred: selling, administrative and general costs, and storage after arrival unless production needs it (para. 16).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary buys 600 joints of casing from an overseas mill, FOB shipping point, list price USD 2.50 thousand per joint, with a 4 percent trade discount.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Invoice = 600 × 2.50 × (1 − 4%) = 1,440","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost at 31 December, at sea = 1,440 + 72 ocean freight and insurance = 1,512","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost on arrival = 1,512 + 18 non-recoverable import charges + 30 port handling and trucking = 1,560","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash discounts for prompt payment follow one of two methods. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"gross method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" records the invoice amount and deducts the discount only when earned. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"net method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" records the discounted price from the start, on the view that it is the real price, so a missed discount is a finance cost under other income and expense. Suppose terms of 2/10, net 30, so paying within ten days saves 1,440 × 2% = 28.8.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross method","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net method","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Purchase","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventories 1,440 / Cr Trade payables 1,440","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventories 1,411.2 / Cr Trade payables 1,411.2","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid within 10 days","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 1,440 / Cr Inventories 28.8 / Cr Cash 1,411.2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 1,411.2 / Cr Cash 1,411.2","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid late","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 1,440 / Cr Cash 1,440","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Trade payables 1,411.2, Dr Purchase discounts lost 28.8 / Cr Cash 1,440","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid on time, both reach a shelf cost of 1,560 − 28.8 = 1,531.2, or USD 2.552 thousand per joint. Paid late, the gross method leaves 28.8 inside the casing, while the net method shows it as the cost of financing the purchase. Kieso prefers the net method. PSAK 202 does not settle the choice: its interest rule (para. 18) covers settlement beyond normal credit terms, and day 30 of net 30 is within them. IFRS practice deducts discounts taken and generally leaves a missed discount in cost, the gross-method result.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Cost formulas","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When identical items are bought at different prices, which price leaves with an issue? A cost formula answers that. It does not change the total; it only splits one pool of cost between what is used up and what stays on the shelf. PSAK 202 allows three answers (paras. 23–25).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Specific identification","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" tracks each item's actual cost. It is required for items that are not interchangeable or are set aside for one project, such as a wellhead built for one well.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"First-in, first-out (FIFO)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" assumes the oldest items go first, so closing stock carries the latest prices.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Weighted average","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" gives every unit the pool's average cost, once for the period (periodic) or after every purchase (moving average, in a perpetual system).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary's tubing ledger for one year, in USD thousand per joint:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 January: on hand 200 joints at 1.20 = 240.00","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10 March: buy 300 joints at 1.30 = 390.00","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15 May: issue 350 joints to a routine workover (a repair of an existing well)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5 September: buy 250 joints at 1.45 = 362.50","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"20 November: issue 150 joints to a new development well","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost available = 992.50 for 750 joints; closing stock 250 joints","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"FIFO: issues = 200 × 1.20 + 150 × 1.30 + 150 × 1.30 = 630.00; closing = 250 × 1.45 = 362.50","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Periodic average: 992.50 / 750 = 1.32333; issues = 500 × 1.32333 = 661.67; closing = 250 × 1.32333 = 330.83","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Moving average: after March (240.00 + 390.00) / 500 = 1.26, so May issue = 441.00 and 189.00 remains; after September (189.00 + 362.50) / 400 = 1.37875, so November issue = 206.81 and closing = 344.69","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All three split the same 992.50. With prices rising, FIFO leaves the most on the shelf and the least in expense; the averages sit in between. LIFO would leave the oldest prices on the shelf (200 × 1.20 + 50 × 1.30 = 305.00, periodic), which is why IAS 2 removed it in 2003, because in a long inflation the balance sheet would carry prices from decades ago. Indonesian income tax law is narrower still and accepts only average and FIFO (UU PPh art. 10(6)).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The same formula must be used for inventories of similar nature and use across a group (paras. 25–26). An issue lands according to what the material does: the routine workover keeps a well producing, so its cost is a production expense, while the development well is a new asset, so its cost is capitalised into oil and gas properties and reaches profit later through depletion on a unit-of-production basis (para. 35). PHE uses the weighted average (Note 3j).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Lower of cost and net realisable value","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost says what was spent to get an item onto the shelf, but not whether that spending will come back. The lower of cost and NRV rule closes that gap. An asset should not be carried at more than the entity expects to recover from it (para. 9), and a loss that has already happened belongs in this period.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NRV = estimated selling price in the ordinary course of business − estimated costs to complete − estimated costs to sell","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"There are only two possibilities. If NRV is above cost, nothing happens. The rule only writes down, and an expected gain waits for the sale. If NRV is below cost, the item is written down to NRV and the difference is expensed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Unit of comparison.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" The comparison is made item by item. Similar items may be grouped only when they cannot practicably be judged one by one, and writing down a whole class such as \"all spares\" is not allowed (para. 29). For example (illustration), suppose 51,000 of PHE's 852,308 of materials at cost (Note 11) is surplus that SKK Migas has approved for sale, so each item has a selling price.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"NRV","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lower of the two","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Drill pipe, 5-inch, grade G","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11,200","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11,200","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Drill pipe, 5-inch, grade S","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gate valves, 3-inch","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8,100","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8,100","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"51,000","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"51,100","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"49,300","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item by item: write-down = 51,000 − 49,300 = 1,700. As a whole: NRV 51,100 > cost 51,000, no write-down","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Comparing totals would let 1,800 of headroom on grade S pipe hide 1,700 of losses on the other two items.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Materials held for use.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Most of PHE's stock is never sold, so there is no selling price to use. PSAK 202 looks at what the material turns into. Materials held for use in production are not written down below cost while the product they go into is expected to sell at or above cost (para. 32), because the material's value is recovered through the product. For an upstream group the \"product\" is the oil and gas the field produces, or the well the material builds. That is a reading by analogy, since that output never sits in inventory. Only when the product no longer covers its cost is the material written down, and replacement cost may then be the best available measure of its NRV. For example (illustration), drilling chemicals bought for 1,000 whose replacement price has fallen to 850.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Field still economic: no write-down, carried at 1,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Field no longer covers its cost: write-down = 1,000 − 850 = 150","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At an upstream group the test therefore bites mainly on stock that will not be used at all: obsolete grades, parts for retired equipment, materials that have sat too long (para. 28). PHE describes its allowance in those terms, for \"obsolete, unuseable and slow-moving materials\" (Note 3j).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Recording the write-down.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Kieso gives two methods that differ only in where the expense sits. The ","type":"text"},{"text":"COGS method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" folds it into cost of goods sold, for PHE production expenses; the ","type":"text"},{"text":"loss method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" shows it on its own line. Either way most entities credit an allowance, a contra account, so the item ledger stays at cost and the balance sheet shows the net figure. That is where the line gets the \"net\" in its name. Take PHE's 2024 numbers as the starting point (Note 11): cost 852,308, allowance needed at year end 45,911, opening allowance 37,639. Assume, for illustration, that the whole movement is new write-downs with no scrapping or reversals.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net charge for 2024 = 45,911 − 37,639 = 8,272; inventories, net = 852,308 − 45,911 = 806,397","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"COGS method","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Loss method","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Production expenses 8,272","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Loss due to decline in inventory value 8,272","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cr Allowance for decline in value 8,272","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cr Allowance for decline in value 8,272","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Profit and the net 806,397 are the same under both. PHE does not say which line carries the charge.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Recovery and scrapping.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When the reason for a write-down goes away, it is reversed, but only up to the original write-down, so an item never returns above cost (para. 33), and the reversal reduces the period's inventory expense (para. 34). For example (illustration), in 2025, slow-moving pipe carrying an allowance of 4,000 is taken up for a new well campaign.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Allowance for decline in value 4,000 / Cr Recovery of inventory loss 4,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If the pipe were scrapped at the value already provided for, cost and allowance would leave together (Dr Allowance and Dr Cash for any scrap proceeds / Cr Inventories) and profit would not move.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Other valuation bases and estimation methods","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Purchase commitments.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A loss can arise before the goods arrive. Kieso recognises it when the market price falls below a non-cancellable contract price. PSAK reaches it through PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37): a contract is ","type":"text"},{"text":"onerous","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" when the unavoidable cost of meeting it exceeds the benefit expected from it (para. 10). For example (illustration), a subsidiary has a non-cancellable order for casing at 10,000 for 2025 delivery, and by the end of 2024 the market price is 7,000. If the casing is still going into economic wells, section 6 says it would not be written down on delivery, so nothing is booked. If the drilling programme has been cancelled, a provision of 3,000 is recognised.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Loss on onerous purchase commitment 3,000 / Cr Provision 3,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On delivery: Dr Inventories 7,000, Dr Provision 3,000 / Cr Cash 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Basket purchases.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Items bought for one price share it by their ","type":"text"},{"text":"relative standalone sales value","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". For example (illustration), a lot of used rig spares bought for 900, with standalone values of 600 for pumps and 300 each for valves and fittings (1,200 in total).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Pumps = 900 × 600 / 1,200 = 450; valves = fittings = 900 × 300 / 1,200 = 225","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Inventories, pumps 450, Dr Inventories, valves 225, Dr Inventories, fittings 225 / Cr Cash 900","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The gross profit method.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When stock cannot be counted, after a fire or for an interim report, it is estimated from the normal margin. For example (illustration), opening stock 800, purchases 3,600, sales 5,000, normal gross margin 25 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cost of goods sold = 5,000 × 75% = 3,750; closing inventory = 800 + 3,600 − 3,750 = 650","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"It rests on a past margin that may no longer hold, so Kieso treats it as an estimate or a check, not a basis for annual statements.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The retail method.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Retailers apply the same logic through a ratio of cost to retail price, accepted where many fast-moving items share similar margins (para. 22). ","type":"text"},{"text":"Markups","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" raise retail prices above the original and ","type":"text"},{"text":"markdowns","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" cut them below it. For example (illustration), a retail subsidiary has opening stock of 50 at cost and 100 at retail, purchases of 2,000 at cost and 3,500 at retail, net markups of 200, net markdowns of 50 and sales of 2,500.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Goods available: cost 2,050; retail 3,600 + 200 markups = 3,800","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ratio A (markups only) = 2,050 / 3,800 = 53.95%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Retail after markdowns = 3,800 − 50 = 3,750; ratio B = 2,050 / 3,750 = 54.67%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing stock at retail = 3,750 − 2,500 = 1,250","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At cost: A = 1,250 × 53.95% = 674.3; B = 1,250 × 54.67% = 683.3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ratio A leaves markdowns out of the denominator, so it gives the lower closing stock, the conventional version that approximates the lower of cost and market.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Errors.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Closing inventory is subtracted to get the expense, so overstating it by 1,000 makes this year's profit 1,000 too high and, as next year's opening stock, next year's 1,000 too low. The error reverses over two years, but both years are misstated. A material error found later is corrected retrospectively under PSAK 208 (formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Inventory across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Manufacturing.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Cost includes ","type":"text"},{"text":"conversion costs","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": direct labour and a systematic share of production overhead (para. 12). Fixed overhead is spread on ","type":"text"},{"text":"normal capacity","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", not actual output, so a quiet year does not make each unit look dearer (para. 13). In an abnormally busy year the rate is reduced instead, so units are not carried above cost, and variable overhead follows actual use. For example (illustration), fixed overhead 1,200, normal capacity 1,000 units, actual output 800.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rate = 1,200 / 1,000 = 1.2 per unit; into inventory = 800 × 1.2 = 960; expensed as idle capacity = 1,200 − 960 = 240","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Standard costs may be used if they approximate actual cost and are reviewed regularly (para. 21).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Agriculture.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Living animals and plants are ","type":"text"},{"text":"biological assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" under PSAK 241 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Agriculture","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 69, IAS 41), not inventory. They are measured at fair value less costs to sell (not NRV), with each period's change in profit or loss, because growth itself is how value is created. At harvest the produce becomes inventory, and its fair value less costs to sell at that moment becomes its cost under PSAK 202 (para. 20). Plants that only bear produce, such as oil palms, rubber or tea, are ","type":"text"},{"text":"bearer plants","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" and are fixed assets under PSAK 216; the produce growing on them stays in PSAK 241 until harvest. For example (illustration), a plantation harvests 10,000 tonnes of fresh fruit bunches when their fair value is 0.19 per tonne (USD 190) and costs to sell are 0.01 per tonne. The produce on the trees was carried at 1,500 at the last reporting date.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fair value less costs to sell at harvest = 10,000 × (0.19 − 0.01) = 1,800","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Remeasure to harvest: Dr Biological assets 300 / Cr Gain on change in fair value less costs to sell 300","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Harvest: Dr Inventories, fresh fruit bunches 1,800 / Cr Biological assets 1,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 300 gain at harvest (IAS 41.28) recognises growth as it happens. From there the bunches are ordinary inventory, so milling into crude palm oil adds conversion costs to the 1,800, and the lower of cost and NRV applies.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Producers of minerals and crops, and commodity traders.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PSAK 202 exempts two groups from its measurement rules (para. 3). Producers of agricultural and forest products, of produce after harvest, and of minerals and mineral products may measure at NRV where that is established industry practice, for example when sale is assured by a forward contract or a government guarantee, or an active market exists (para. 4). Commodity broker-traders, who buy to profit from short-term price moves, may measure at fair value less costs to sell (para. 5), in both cases with changes in profit or loss.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Mining.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Ore stockpiles and concentrate are inventory at the lower of cost and NRV. In an open-pit mine the cost of removing waste rock during production is split between inventory, for ore extracted now, and a ","type":"text"},{"text":"stripping activity asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", for access to ore later (ISAK 120, formerly ISAK 29, IFRIC 20). In Indonesia this replaced PSAK 33, which was revoked from 2014.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Real estate.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Land and buildings a developer holds for sale are inventory (para. 8). The same building would be investment property under PSAK 240 (formerly PSAK 13, IAS 40) if held for rent or capital appreciation, and a fixed asset under PSAK 216 if used by the entity. Intention decides the classification.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Financial instruments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" are never inventory under PSAK 202 (para. 2(b)), even for a securities dealer; they follow PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 11","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 202 asks for the policy, the carrying amount by classification, the amount expensed, write-downs and reversals, and stock pledged (para. 36). PHE gives gross cost, the allowance and its movement (Note 11, Daftar 5/99, PDF p. 116).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"General materials, at cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"852,308","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"696,351","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Less allowance for decline in value","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(45,911)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(37,639)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Inventories, net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"806,397","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"658,712","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allowance coverage = 37,639 / 696,351 = 5.41% (2023); 45,911 / 852,308 = 5.39% (2024)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The allowance grew in step with cost, so the 22.4 percent rise in the net line came from more materials at a steady coverage ratio. PHE prints one combined movement a year (−3,084 in 2023, +8,272 in 2024), and one net figure can hide several events. For example (illustration), the 2023 deduction of 3,084 could be all reversals (profit up 3,084), all scrapping of fully provided stock (profit unchanged), or new write-downs of 2,000 with 4,000 scrapped and 1,084 reversed (profit down 916).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Inventory turnover = inventory expensed / average inventory; days in inventory = 365 / turnover","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), if PHE's subsidiaries had used 1,600,000 of materials in 2024, turnover would be 1,600,000 / ((806,397 + 658,712) / 2) = 2.18 times, about 167 days. PHE does not disclose the total amount of inventories expensed. Note 39 gives material usage of 163,694 for its other operations only (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166), and the cash flow statement uses the direct method (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17), so the real ratio cannot be computed. PHE also keeps 6,809 of \"dead stock material\" outside the line, in other non-current assets (Note 14b, Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Upstream note: cost recovery","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under a cost recovery PSC, material costs are recovered from production (Note 2a(iii), Daftar 5/10, PDF p. 27). A deferred tax liability on \"non-capital inventory\" of 17,193 (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172) suggests such materials are deducted for PSC purposes before issue. Gross split contracts have no cost recovery (Note 2b, Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34). The inventory accounting is the same under both.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"oil-gas-properties-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/oil-gas-properties-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where materials issued to development wells are capitalised.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"fixed-assets-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/fixed-assets-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the boundary for major spares and stand-by equipment.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"production-expenses","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/production-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": issued consumables and, under the COGS method, write-downs.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the other side of purchases.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-non-current-assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/advances-prepayments-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": holds the dead stock material.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the temporary difference on non-capital inventory.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The control argument for carrying government-owned materials is this page's reading. PHE does not explain why it recognises them. That non-capital materials are recovered before they are issued is read from Note 43e, not stated in the FS, and a deferred tax liability can have other causes.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"How PSAK 202 treats a missed settlement discount is not stated in the standard. The reading follows the 2004 IFRIC discussion and firm guidance.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Applying the PSAK 202 materials rule (para. 32) to an upstream group, where the product never sits in inventory, is a reading by analogy, as is using it to decide that the casing commitment is not onerous.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That little crude would be held at year end is inferred from the revenue policy. The FS state no policy for crude inventory.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); cash flow statement (Daftar 4, PDF p. 17); Note 2a(iii) and 2a(vii) (Daftar 5/10–5/11, PDF pp. 27–28); Note 2b (Daftar 5/17, PDF p. 34); Note 3j (Daftar 5/53, PDF p. 70); Note 3p (Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79); Note 11 (Daftar 5/99–5/100, PDF pp. 116–117); Note 14b (Daftar 5/105, PDF p. 122); Note 39 (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 1 – Chapter 8 – Valuation of Inventories: A Cost-Basis Approach\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): classification, perpetual and periodic systems, inventory control, goods included, product and period costs, gross and net methods. NOTES. The notes do not cover cost formulas or inventory errors; section 5 and the errors paragraph follow Kieso (ref. 8) and IAS 2.23–27.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes, \"Inter 1 – Chapter 9 – Inventories: Additional Valuation Issues\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): lower of cost and NRV, COGS and loss methods, allowance, recovery, agricultural inventory, purchase commitments, relative standalone sales value, gross profit and retail methods. NOTES. Of section 8, only agriculture has a note; the rest follows IAS 2.2–5, 2.8, 2.12–13 and the standards named.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 202 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Persediaan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 2 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Inventories","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), paras. 2–6, 8–13, 16, 18, 20–29, 32–36. VERIFIED (web, IAS 2 text reproduced at prg.kz; Grant Thornton IAS 2 factsheet). Settlement discounts: IFRS Interpretations Committee, \"IAS 2 Discounts and rebates\" (not added to agenda, Nov 2004); Grant Thornton IFRS viewpoint, \"Inventory discounts and rebates\". VERIFIED (web). The 2003 removal of LIFO is from knowledge, NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 241 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Agrikultur","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 41 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Agriculture","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":") and the 2014 bearer plants amendments to IAS 16 and IAS 41. Scope VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation, \"Agriculture: Bearer Plants\", June 2014); IAS 41.12–13 and 28 VERIFIED (web, IAS Plus; PKF; ACCA DipIFR). The FFB price is an illustration, not a market figure.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 216 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Aset Tetap","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 16), para. 8; PSAK 201 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Penyajian Laporan Keuangan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 1), paras. 66(a), 68. VERIFIED (web, Ind AS 16 mirror; ifrscommunity.com).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 (IAS 37), paras. 10, 68–69: VERIFIED (web, IFRS Foundation 2020 amendment \"Onerous Contracts\"). ISAK 120 (IFRIC 20) and the revocation of PSAK 33 by PPSAK 12 from 2014: VERIFIED (web, IAI). PSAK 240 (IAS 40), PSAK 115 (IFRS 15), PSAK 109 (IFRS 9), PSAK 208 (IAS 8): numbers VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, chs. 8–9, the textbook the lecture notes summarise. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"UU No. 7/1983 on Income Tax, as last amended by UU No. 7/2021 (HPP), art. 10(6). VERIFIED (web, secondary: DDTCNews, 14 Sep 2024).","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of inventory accounting from definition to disclosure, worked through PHE's materials and set beside the industries with their own rules","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["Inventory accounting answers three questions in order: which goods belong to the entity, what they cost, and whether that cost will come back. Control settles the first, the cost elements and a cost formula the second, and the lower of cost and net realisable value (NRV) the third.","A cost formula only divides one pool of cost between the balance sheet and profit or loss. When prices rise, FIFO leaves the most on the balance sheet and weighted average less. LIFO, which leaves the least, is no longer allowed.","Not every industry follows the cost model. Agricultural produce enters inventory at fair value less costs to sell at harvest (PSAK 241), commodity broker-traders may carry stock at fair value less costs to sell, and producers of minerals and crops may carry it at NRV. PHE's materials follow the plain cost model, with an allowance of 45,911 against 852,308 of cost at the end of 2024."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '103737ec-579a-4330-a996-adf381d57bbe';
